WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:08.350
Welcome, everyone,
to today's webinar.

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:12.130
We're going to talk about
reproducible examples

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:15.430
from a conceptual point
of view and why they're

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:18.700
surprisingly important,
and then also a great deal

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.220
from a mechanical
point of view how

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.560
to make your reproducible
examples in a way

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.990
that they're easy to
share with other people.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:36.850
So this short link, the
rstd.io/reprex currently points

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:41.840
to a copy of these
slides on Speaker Deck.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:47.050
Another very relevant URL
is reprex.tidyverse.com,

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.570
which is the website
for the package I'm

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:51.250
going to talk about.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.490
And we may change what
the upper short link links

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:57.760
to as a result of this
webinar because we're

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.830
pretty excited to get some
of these materials captured,

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:03.490
but I promise it will
always point to something

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.310
very relevant to this
package that will link

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:07.780
to absolutely everything else.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.030
And you're also going
to see that short link

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:13.300
many, many times during
this presentation.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.540
And as said in the
intro, this video

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:19.810
will also be posted within 48
hours, and all of these things

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.600
will be publicized.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.650
And this is my license.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:30.270
So the first thing I want
to do is show basic usage.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.160
And we're just going
to get right into it,

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.685
and then we'll unpack
what you just saw.

00:01:34.685 --> 00:01:37.960
So I'm actually going
to leave my slides

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.130
and go over to RStudio.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:44.160
So I'm sitting here in
an R studio session.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.030
It's fresh, and I have
a little bit of code

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.020
up here in my source editor.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:55.070
I'm going to make a
factor x, a factor y.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:59.000
I'm going to combine them
and get what, to most of us,

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.300
is kind of a puzzling
result. So this is just

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:06.290
going to be an example
of a small piece of code

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:10.340
that maybe you want to talk
about on the community site

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.100
or share with your
local R expert and ask,

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.850
what's going on?

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.310
So this is how you
would use reprex

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.200
to turn this little
snippet of code

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.200
into a reproducible example.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.100
This is the path of
least resistance.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.640
We'll talk about
other methods later.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:30.780
So I would select a
little piece of code

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.480
and copy it to my clipboard.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:44.380
Then over in the R console
I'm going to type reprex.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:50.050
And you will see that that
little piece of code is run,

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:53.350
and then basically a beautiful,
attractive version of that

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:58.790
is stored on my clipboard,
and I can preview it here.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:04.610
So if I were to paste the
contents of my clipboard

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:09.660
right now, you actually
see what's called markdown.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.100
And this is what's necessary to
create the attractive version

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.900
of this code.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:16.980
And why is this helpful?

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:23.970
Because you can go to places
like GitHub, the R studio

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.670
community site,
or Stack Overflow

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.900
and paste this markdown in.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.400
So I'm going to show
you what this would

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.970
look like in a GitHub issue.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:39.160
So that's that same
markdown that you just saw.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.900
GitHub lets you preview
things, and you'll

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:45.050
see that it looks just the
way it did locally for me.

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:46.100
It's been rendered.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.780
It's syntax highlighted.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.970
We have a tiny
little ad down here

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:52.130
that tells people
how you did this.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:56.320
And I could submit
that as a GitHub issue.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:01.630
So that is the basic process.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:05.010
And the reason I
can just type reprex

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:11.230
is that I always have
this package attached.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:12.970
And so you might
need to call this,

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:16.000
and we're going to talk a
great deal about that next.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.990
So that is what basic
reprex usage looks like.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:24.320
It creates a small
little piece of code,

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.250
renders it nicely, and
it's ready to paste

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:31.350
into other formats.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:37.490
So this is just a static
version of what we just did.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:41.820
And this is the GIF that I
use on the reprex website.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:43.370
I rewatched this clip.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.125
It's from a movie
called Jerry Maguire.

00:04:45.125 --> 00:04:48.170
It's still highly recommended.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:51.770
And basically the reason for
bothering to do all of this

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:55.800
is if you're going somewhere
to have a conversation about R,

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:02.300
to have questions answered, or
to describe a bug in software,

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:06.320
being careful about how you make
your reproducible example makes

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:11.020
it much, much, much easier
for other people to help you.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:14.670
And I want to explain where
this word came from, reprex.

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.440
So Romain first
tweeted this, and I

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.990
thought it was just
a great made-up word.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:24.250
So it is short for
reproducible example.

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:31.040
So it is a completely made-up
word, but it's just very handy.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.010
And I'm going to use the word
reprex over and over and over

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:38.420
again in this webinar, so I
want to be very clear that I'm

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:43.170
using it in, I'd say, three
distinct but related ways.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:48.650
So I think people, at least
in the small R community,

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:51.380
are starting to say
reprex just as a noun,

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:53.940
like it is a
reproducible example.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.360
And that has nothing
to do with whether you

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:58.040
use this package or not.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:59.990
But then today's
webinar is going

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:05.090
to show you use of a package
with that same name, reprex,

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:07.010
that you can install from CRAN.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:09.780
I'll show you how to do
that in just a moment.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.230
And then this is a
pretty small package.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:16.670
It has a couple functions, that
really the main function it has

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:19.040
is also called reprex.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.290
So this webinar is
going to talk about how

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:26.210
to use the reprex function
inside the reprex package

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:29.250
to produce a good-looking
reproducible example.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:34.940
And when does this
come up in your life?

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:37.090
It's very handy
for conversations

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:42.370
that you have on
community.rstudio.com.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:46.540
It's very handy for preparing
questions or answers

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:49.580
for Stack Overflow.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:54.440
It's very handy for reporting
bugs or making feature requests

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:59.150
for an R package that is
developed on GitHub and also

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:03.650
very useful for having
detailed conversations about R

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:05.830
in Slack or in email.

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:09.080
So a reproducible
example conceptually

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:16.160
is useful in all those places,
and then this package, reprex,

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:20.450
smooths over some
of the mechanics.

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:22.460
So here I want to talk
to you about what you're

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.800
going to need to
do on your computer

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.075
to make this package
available to yourself.

00:07:29.075 --> 00:07:34.760
So reprex does not come with R.
It does not come with RStudio.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:38.490
You have to make an explicit
effort to install it.

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:41.570
So you should pick
one of these methods,

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:46.580
and it's the type of thing
that you do once per computer.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:52.760
So you could use
install.packages("reprex")

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:56.320
to install just
the reprex package.

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:59.470
It is also part
of the metapackage

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:01.300
that we call tidyverse.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:04.630
So if you did
install.packages("tidyverse"),

00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:07.870
reprex would be one of the many
packages that get installed

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:10.210
on your machine.

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:12.460
In general, there
is very little harm

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:16.070
that you can do to yourself
by reinstalling packages.

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:20.270
So you also should not
stress out too much about--

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:23.990
you could install just reprex
and then install the tidyverse

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:25.330
and nothing bad will happen.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:28.800
So do it once per machine
just because that's

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:31.630
the minimum you need to do,
but it's no tragedy if you

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:34.179
reinstall things.

00:08:34.179 --> 00:08:36.190
Once you've installed,
you still need

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:41.740
to use our sort of library call
to make the reprex functions

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:43.730
available in your R session.

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:47.890
So you would need to do
this in every R session

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:50.110
that you plan to use reprex in.

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:51.680
So that might be
something that you

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.220
do multiple times per day,
certainly way, way, way,

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:57.042
way more than once per computer.

00:08:57.042 --> 00:08:58.750
So every time you want
to use the package

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:04.060
you'll need to execute the
library(reprex) command.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:08.670
Now I use reprex several
times a day for the most part,

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:11.240
and so that would
be very annoying.

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:14.560
So an alternative, if you
also become a semiheavy user

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:19.030
of this package, is to make
it available to yourself

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:21.220
all the time.

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:26.320
So you can control the startup
behavior of R through a file

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:27.940
called .Rprofile.

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:31.810
And, conventionally, it's
found in your home directory.

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:35.320
And so this snippet of
code, suppressMessages

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:37.510
(require(reprex)),
looks a little different

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:41.080
from what you just saw, but
it's sort of a better way

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.660
to attach this package
in your startup file.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:49.630
So you would put this snippet
of code there in your profile.

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:53.470
And then forever more
when you start R,

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:56.600
the reprex package
will be available.

00:09:56.600 --> 00:10:00.760
And if you have never thought
about your Rprofile file

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:05.350
before, there is a function in
the usethis package, which you

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:08.920
would also have to install, that
will create it for you if you

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:11.270
don't have it, or
if you do have it,

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:14.932
it would open it
for you for editing

00:10:14.932 --> 00:10:16.390
in case you wanted
to put something

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:19.937
like this snippet in there.

00:10:19.937 --> 00:10:21.520
So once you've done
those two things--

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:25.390
you've installed it and you've
attached it through either

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:29.140
the library command or
by putting something

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:30.520
in your .Rprofile--

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:33.700
you are ready to use
the reprex function.

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:36.700
And what you saw me do
in that first demo was

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:40.720
I actually called the
reprex function explicitly

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:42.490
in the R console.

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:45.075
And something you'll see
me do before we're done

00:10:45.075 --> 00:10:47.710
is we also have put some
what are called RStudio

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:51.070
add-ins into this package
that give you even more

00:10:51.070 --> 00:10:55.870
ways to launch this function.

00:10:55.870 --> 00:11:01.390
And this is a bit of a sidebar,
but since I have shown you

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:04.880
how to put things in
your Rprofile file,

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:08.860
I also want to tell you
how to do that responsibly.

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:11.200
So reprex is a workflow package.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:14.470
It's something you would
use in your daily work

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:16.250
to make your life a
little bit easier.

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:18.310
You use it interactively.

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:23.150
I would be pretty shocked to
see it show up in a typical

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:27.190
person's R scripts, R
markdown files, packages,

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:28.640
or Shiny apps .

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:31.360
And so the fact that it doesn't
show up in those things,

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:33.430
it's an interactive
package, makes

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:37.390
it safe to attach
in your R profile.

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:39.370
But I don't want you
to get the wrong idea

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:42.820
and think, oh my God, I should
do this with all my packages.

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:47.530
So I would not want to see
this kind of code using

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:51.630
dplyr or ggplot2 or things that
do show up in your scripts.

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:56.170
And it's because your scripts
would then become highly not

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:57.290
self-contained.

00:11:57.290 --> 00:11:59.980
And they would work for
you because of stuff

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:02.660
in your Rprofile, but they
won't work for other people.

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:05.360
So this is an interesting
technique to know about,

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:06.790
but you need to
be really, really

00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:09.700
careful about what you do here.

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:12.070
So I think it's safe
to put reprex in there.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:16.280
It is not safe to
put dplyr in there.

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:18.410
OK, so we're going to
get back to the package

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:20.600
and to reproducible
examples now.

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:23.270
So I wanted to
give a brief intro,

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:26.210
like what on earth in
my life drove me to make

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:29.480
this one of my missions?

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:32.450
Before I joined RStudio,
I was a professor

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:35.060
at the University
of British Columbia,

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:40.670
and I had a course called Stat
454 that has a lot of content

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:41.960
online to this day.

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:42.980
The course continues.

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:44.540
You could go there.

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:48.170
And I ran this course
entirely on GitHub.

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:53.030
And so it meant that all of
my dialogue with students,

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:56.660
both sort of me to
the whole class and me

00:12:56.660 --> 00:13:01.610
talking to individual students,
took place in GitHub issues.

00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:05.340
And I actually analyzed my
GitHub usage in the course

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:08.150
and I found that
every fall I was

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:14.420
participating in at least 300,
maybe 500 GitHub issue threads.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:17.720
And that's just in
my teaching life.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:21.230
So I spend a great deal of time
talking about R in those places

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:24.650
and solving people's R problems.

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:28.760
And to do that well, I actually
wanted to use executable R

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:33.100
code, and the friction involved
in making that look good

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:35.530
started to drive me crazy.

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:40.040
And now that I'm no longer
a full-time faculty member

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:43.070
and I'm working full
time on R packages,

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:46.430
this just gives you a sense
of the intensity of my GitHub

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:48.050
activity over the last year.

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:52.090
So now I still work with tons
and tons of GitHub issues

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:55.575
in a different capacity,
and then I also talk about R

00:13:55.575 --> 00:13:56.790
a lot in Slack.

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:00.560
So I still have this
sort of hourly need

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:04.280
to run little pieces
of R code and share

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:05.820
what I'm seeing
with other people.

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:13.370
So trying to remove friction
for myself and other people

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:18.050
lead me to create a few
principles that I knew

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:20.370
had to be true of a tool
to make this easier.

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:24.500
So this is the
reprex philosophy.

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:27.140
I think that
conversations about code

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:31.440
are much more productive if
they contain three things--

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:34.130
well, it's one thing
but three properties--

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:39.770
code that actually
runs; code that I do not

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:46.760
have to run as the reader but
code that I can easily run.

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:50.550
And so there is a little bit
of self-contradiction here,

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:53.450
but the point is you want
to make it easy for people

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:56.402
to interact with your
reproducible example

00:14:56.402 --> 00:14:57.860
in a whole bunch
of different ways.

00:14:57.860 --> 00:14:59.390
They can just be a consumer.

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:01.790
They can just read
it, or they can easily

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:06.020
grab it and run it themselves,
modify it, and share that back

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:07.790
with you.

00:15:07.790 --> 00:15:11.030
So I want to be really
detailed about what I mean when

00:15:11.030 --> 00:15:13.110
I say code that actually runs.

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:15.980
So you're going to isolate
a little piece of R code

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:17.955
and you hand it off to reprex.

00:15:17.955 --> 00:15:18.830
You've seen one demo.

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:21.080
We're about to do
a whole bunch more.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:27.050
That code is taken and it is run
in a completely new R session,

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:31.220
and that means it has to be
completely self-contained.

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:35.390
So it must include the command
to load all necessary packages,

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:38.630
and it must create
all necessary objects.

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:40.880
And this can be very
frustrating, for people

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:43.940
but it's extremely important.

00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:46.610
So I'm going to go
do this live just

00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:49.630
to show exactly what I mean.

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:59.350
So I'm looking at an R script
that contains the code you just

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:01.340
saw in that slide.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:04.820
And I'm going to restart R.

00:16:04.820 --> 00:16:08.060
So let's imagine a typical
interactive R session.

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:09.980
So I'm going to be down
in the console here

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:13.100
and I'm going to
say, oh, I'd like

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:15.860
to play a little
bit with this praise

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:17.180
package I've heard about.

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:18.500
So there I go.

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:22.780
I say library("praise")
done in the console.

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:26.520
Now up in my source
editor I make

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:31.250
a new object called template,
and it's a template string.

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:34.410
Exclamation, your
reprex is adjective.

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:38.199
And so if I then call
the praise function

00:16:38.199 --> 00:16:39.240
from the praise package--

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:40.573
I don't expect you to know this.

00:16:40.573 --> 00:16:42.690
I'm just using it
as an example--

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:45.030
it's going to create
random little sentences

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:49.280
for us praising someone
for their awesome reprex.

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:52.040
So let's say I want to share
my joy about this with people.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:54.740
Using the reprex
package, I would select

00:16:54.740 --> 00:16:56.750
this little snippet of code.

00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:59.760
Again, this is the long way.

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:01.330
I'll show you a short way later.

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:02.470
Copy it.

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:05.960
Go down to the console.

00:17:05.960 --> 00:17:09.846
Type reprex and hit Return.

00:17:09.846 --> 00:17:12.940
And now let's look
at the preview here.

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:17.369
It shows defining template,
and then my praise call fails.

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:18.609
Error in praise.

00:17:18.609 --> 00:17:22.450
Could not find function praise.

00:17:22.450 --> 00:17:27.380
And that's because you don't
have the library praise

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:28.950
command here.

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:32.150
So over in that fresh R
session, the praise package

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:34.750
is not available to use.

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:36.730
So here's something
else you might do.

00:17:36.730 --> 00:17:40.010
You're like, OK, I'm
going to add that command.

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:42.890
Then I'm going to make
my call to the package.

00:17:42.890 --> 00:17:44.410
Let's see if that works.

00:17:44.410 --> 00:17:46.680
Copy.

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:51.800
Call reprex again,
and a new error.

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:53.990
Error in grep whatever,
whatever, object

00:17:53.990 --> 00:17:56.000
template not found.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:59.790
So this snippet is incomplete
in a different way.

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:02.450
It actually doesn't
contain the code that

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:05.940
defines the template object.

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:08.190
So here is the full snippet.

00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:12.780
It loads the praise package.

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:16.140
It defines the
template object, and it

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:18.360
makes this function call.

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:24.140
So I'm going to copy all
of that to the clipboard,

00:18:24.140 --> 00:18:32.806
re-execute reprex, and we
have made an exquisite reprex.

00:18:32.806 --> 00:18:35.610
And so that's a
little belabored,

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:39.090
but when I try to answer
R questions for people

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:43.770
and I try to run their code,
the two most common ways

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:49.050
that I fail are that
they haven't explicitly

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:50.970
listed all the
packages they're using

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:54.090
and I have to either
sleuth it out of them

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:57.750
or figure it out for myself
and add those commands,

00:18:57.750 --> 00:19:01.800
or the objects they're referring
to are not available to me.

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:05.770
And so those are the two reasons
why I can't run their code.

00:19:05.770 --> 00:19:12.190
So on the reprex website I have
a list of dos and don'ts that

00:19:12.190 --> 00:19:17.200
are distilled from a lot of
other really fantastic sources

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:19.840
about creating reproducible
examples which are referenced

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:20.680
there.

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:23.230
But the three big,
big high points

00:19:23.230 --> 00:19:27.310
are you need to write this
reproducible example using

00:19:27.310 --> 00:19:30.960
the smallest, the simplest,
and the most built-in data

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:33.370
set you can get away with.

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:35.520
And that is very
uncomfortable for people.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:38.220
I'm going to talk
about that in a second.

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:43.910
Include commands on a ruthlessly
strict, need-to-run basis.

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:47.700
And you really need to
strip your example down.

00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:50.220
And then I say pack
it in, pack it out,

00:19:50.220 --> 00:19:52.980
and don't take liberties with
other people's computers.

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:58.170
And this is referring to making
sure that if you create files,

00:19:58.170 --> 00:20:00.880
you remove them, or if you
change the working directory,

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:02.160
you reset it.

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:04.180
If you change options,
you reset them,

00:20:04.180 --> 00:20:08.190
but basically leaving
things as you found them.

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.710
But I want to talk about--

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:13.740
so let's see, here's
what that web page

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:18.540
would look like if you
want to read it more.

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:21.420
But let me just give a
short example of something

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:23.730
that a lot of people
struggle with which

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:26.550
is that they feel like they
have some big, hairy data object

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:30.510
and they can only show
their example using it.

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:32.520
So tricks to know--

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:35.730
so the read.csv file you
probably think of as normally

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:39.930
being a function that
you use to bring data--

00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:42.210
sorry, the read.csv function
is something you usually

00:20:42.210 --> 00:20:46.280
bring delimited
data in from a file,

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:48.670
but it also has a
text argument that

00:20:48.670 --> 00:20:53.350
allows you to inline
really tiny R objects,

00:20:53.350 --> 00:20:56.810
and then also just the
data.frame function itself.

00:20:56.810 --> 00:21:03.130
So I'm going to reprex, using
a keyboard shortcut, those two

00:21:03.130 --> 00:21:04.120
snippets of code.

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:08.150
And see, that's a very easy way
to make a very tiny data frame

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:14.020
either inline using read.csv or
sort of from first principles

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:16.200
using data.frame.

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:19.620
And then if you are
a tidyverse adherent,

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:22.260
the tibble package
is what takes care

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:25.500
of the care and feeding
of tibbles, which

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:27.990
are a flavor of data frame.

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:30.390
And the tribble
function is extremely

00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:34.020
useful for creating tiny little
data frames because it allows

00:21:34.020 --> 00:21:39.840
you to write it in this
really humane, row-wise way,

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:43.750
like the same way it would look,
for example, in a CSV file.

00:21:43.750 --> 00:21:47.400
So if I reprex this
little snippet,

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:49.209
we'll see very,
very similar output

00:21:49.209 --> 00:21:51.000
as what we just saw
with the base function,

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:54.390
but it allows you to inline
the creation, in this case,

00:21:54.390 --> 00:21:57.370
of a two-row,
two-column data frame,

00:21:57.370 --> 00:22:01.120
or you can, again, use just
the tibble function directly.

00:22:01.120 --> 00:22:04.440
And so if you make
a lot of reprexes,

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:06.420
you get really good
at figuring out

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:10.740
how to inline the creation
of very small objects.

00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:14.550
Of course figuring out what's
the smallest object that still

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:16.920
shows your problem is
difficult, and we're

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:20.930
going to talk about
that at the very end.

00:22:20.930 --> 00:22:23.690
Another principle is that the
reprex should contain code

00:22:23.690 --> 00:22:28.040
I do not have to run because
a lot of your readers

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:31.160
have a great deal
of R experience

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:34.760
and sometimes-- not always--
but sometimes they can quickly

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:38.060
see the point without
actually running the code,

00:22:38.060 --> 00:22:42.110
but that is greatly enhanced if
they can see the output instead

00:22:42.110 --> 00:22:47.060
of having to run it in their
head and in their imagination

00:22:47.060 --> 00:22:49.170
and try to figure
out what's happening.

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:53.810
It's just much easier if you
can actually see the output.

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:55.370
So that's why I
think it's important

00:22:55.370 --> 00:22:58.640
that your typical
reprex contains the code

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:05.000
and it also reveals the output
being produced by that code.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:10.030
So here's an example I took
from the GitHub repository

00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:14.170
where the readr package
is developed because it's

00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:16.600
a perfect little
example, and it probably

00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:18.660
was produced with reprex.

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:19.980
You can't tell.

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:23.050
And this person is
just reporting a bug,

00:23:23.050 --> 00:23:25.690
but it's like a great
minimal example.

00:23:25.690 --> 00:23:31.150
It says if the header in your
CSV contains coded newlines,

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:34.960
you get kind of a weird column
name and you get weird data.

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:39.130
And the fact that this person
provided a small example

00:23:39.130 --> 00:23:43.450
and it completely shows
the problem I imagine

00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:45.490
is what allowed the
maintainer, Jim Hester,

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:50.050
who's listening to this call,
to quickly label this as a bug.

00:23:50.050 --> 00:23:52.630
And we've already got at
least one other user giving it

00:23:52.630 --> 00:23:55.640
a thumbs up, meaning they've
experienced it as well.

00:23:55.640 --> 00:23:58.810
And so if you would only
have the code here I,

00:23:58.810 --> 00:24:02.050
think you'd have a lot less
sort of quick engagement

00:24:02.050 --> 00:24:02.815
with this issue.

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:06.950
And so I want to show you--

00:24:10.180 --> 00:24:14.050
so code that I can easily
run is very important.

00:24:14.050 --> 00:24:16.940
We're going to keep
working with that issue.

00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:22.400
So if that person had
instead copied and pasted

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:24.140
the output from
their R console, this

00:24:24.140 --> 00:24:26.310
is what we would be faced with.

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:30.810
So if I were Jim Hester and I
needed to reproduce this issue

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:33.660
and make sure that
it's still a problem,

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:37.190
I have a lot of really
annoying editing to do.

00:24:37.190 --> 00:24:39.080
So I have to get rid
of all the prompts

00:24:39.080 --> 00:24:40.940
at the beginning of the lines.

00:24:40.940 --> 00:24:43.910
I have to get rid
of all this output

00:24:43.910 --> 00:24:47.730
to isolate the three lines of
code that actually do anything.

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:49.910
So copy, paste
from the R console

00:24:49.910 --> 00:24:53.090
hits some of our checklist,
but it's not great

00:24:53.090 --> 00:24:58.700
because it's very hard for the
next person to run this code.

00:24:58.700 --> 00:25:02.240
Worse than copy, paste
is the screenshot.

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:06.380
So this of course does, again,
hit some of our checklist.

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:08.530
It clearly shows the
code and the output.

00:25:08.530 --> 00:25:10.400
But again, if
somebody else wanted

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:13.550
to check this and
reproduce it, they actually

00:25:13.550 --> 00:25:16.970
have to retype everything
which, frankly, is never

00:25:16.970 --> 00:25:19.100
going to happen.

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:22.370
And so this is what I
want to see in a reprex

00:25:22.370 --> 00:25:25.350
because it can be
copy, pasted and run.

00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:28.350
So I'm going to prove
that to you right now.

00:25:28.350 --> 00:25:34.100
So if I go to this issue
on GitHub and I copy--

00:25:34.100 --> 00:25:35.880
I could copy all of this.

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:39.060
As long as I get all
the commands, I'm OK.

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:41.410
So I'm going to put
that on my clipboard.

00:25:41.410 --> 00:25:50.620
I'm going to go back to R. Maybe
to make this really explicit,

00:25:50.620 --> 00:25:51.910
I'll show you what I copied.

00:25:51.910 --> 00:25:53.660
All right, that's what I did.

00:25:53.660 --> 00:26:03.920
So I can copy this
again and call reprex,

00:26:03.920 --> 00:26:09.200
and I get exactly what this
person was reporting on GitHub.

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:11.630
So I've been able to
reproduce it very quickly

00:26:11.630 --> 00:26:13.990
from a copy, paste.

00:26:13.990 --> 00:26:16.870
But as you saw, reprex
is like, are you sure

00:26:16.870 --> 00:26:21.220
you want to do this because
I've got this output here.

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:29.350
And so if you really want
to get really clean code

00:26:29.350 --> 00:26:31.960
from a reprex that
someone else has made,

00:26:31.960 --> 00:26:37.150
you capture it and use
one of the undo functions

00:26:37.150 --> 00:26:38.710
in the reprex package.

00:26:38.710 --> 00:26:44.120
I could use a reprex_clean, and
I'll show you that right now.

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:45.650
So here's what I
copied from GitHub.

00:26:45.650 --> 00:26:53.480
So I could copy that
and call reprex_clean.

00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:56.810
And now if I paste,
you'll see all the output

00:26:56.810 --> 00:26:58.950
has been eliminated.

00:26:58.950 --> 00:27:02.720
And so I think that's a
slightly obscure thing

00:27:02.720 --> 00:27:05.750
you might want to
do, but there are

00:27:05.750 --> 00:27:09.660
the full set of sort of
backwards functions in reprex.

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:12.920
So it helps you take
code that people

00:27:12.920 --> 00:27:16.250
have copied from the console
or that they have already

00:27:16.250 --> 00:27:17.450
made a reference from.

00:27:21.940 --> 00:27:26.860
So we've gotten to, essentially,
the meat of the webinar now.

00:27:26.860 --> 00:27:28.870
So if you were really
interested in basic usage,

00:27:28.870 --> 00:27:30.130
you've seen it now.

00:27:30.130 --> 00:27:33.010
And now I'm going to go into
the shock-and-awe section

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:37.420
where I run through a lot
of more-interesting features

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:41.620
of the reprex package that I
still think are pretty cool.

00:27:41.620 --> 00:27:46.425
So the slides show you what
we're about to do live.

00:27:46.425 --> 00:27:49.910
So I'm going back
to RStudio, and I'm

00:27:49.910 --> 00:27:54.020
in a script called shock and
awe where I'm going to restart R

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:56.280
just for good measure.

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:57.950
So the first thing
I want to show you

00:27:57.950 --> 00:28:02.360
is how frictionless
reprex can make it to talk

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:04.460
to people about figures.

00:28:04.460 --> 00:28:09.620
So I'm going to load the
Gapminder data and ggplot2,

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:12.350
and I'm going to make
a plot with ggplot2.

00:28:12.350 --> 00:28:16.020
So you see it down
here in my plots pane.

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:17.790
So let's say that
there's something

00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:19.830
about this I don't
like or that I want

00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:22.020
to discuss with a colleague.

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:23.820
I can use reprex for this.

00:28:23.820 --> 00:28:32.720
So, as usual, I can select the
snippet, copy to my clipboard,

00:28:32.720 --> 00:28:36.730
and run reprex.

00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:40.270
You're going to see
all the same stuff.

00:28:40.270 --> 00:28:42.870
So we've got a nicely
rendered reprex

00:28:42.870 --> 00:28:45.405
that includes the figure.

00:28:45.405 --> 00:28:46.490
But watch this.

00:28:49.940 --> 00:28:53.190
I'm going to go to a GitHub
repo that I created just

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:55.340
to play around with.

00:28:55.340 --> 00:28:58.320
I'm going to create a new issue.

00:28:58.320 --> 00:29:04.385
I have a question about this
plot, and I'm going to paste.

00:29:04.385 --> 00:29:05.760
And let's look at
what we've got.

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:11.290
We have the usual sort of
nicely formatted markdown.

00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:12.930
And look at this.

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:17.910
So when reprex rendered this
code, it made your figure

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:22.710
and pushed it up to Imgur
and dropped this link

00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:25.180
into your markdown.

00:29:25.180 --> 00:29:31.650
So if I submit this
issue, people see my code

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:35.260
and they see the actual
figure that you just made.

00:29:35.260 --> 00:29:39.720
So this is an example of one
of the cool things you can do.

00:29:39.720 --> 00:29:42.450
That removes a tremendous
amount of friction

00:29:42.450 --> 00:29:45.930
if you're trying to have a
quick conversation with somebody

00:29:45.930 --> 00:29:47.500
about code that
produces figures.

00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:52.810
So we're going to go back
to this shock-and-awe script

00:29:52.810 --> 00:29:55.000
and we're going
to execute reprex

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:59.680
many times, showing some of the
options and different arguments

00:29:59.680 --> 00:30:00.810
you have.

00:30:00.810 --> 00:30:05.230
So so far I've only shown
you reprexing when the source

00:30:05.230 --> 00:30:08.560
code is on the clipboard, but
there are a lot of other ways

00:30:08.560 --> 00:30:10.120
to provide the input.

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:14.770
So you can provide it
directly in the reprex call

00:30:14.770 --> 00:30:18.250
as an expression.

00:30:18.250 --> 00:30:22.380
So here you see that the
assignment of x and y

00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:26.140
gets done, and we compute
the correlation between them.

00:30:26.140 --> 00:30:29.310
There's also an input argument,
that I'm actually not going

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:34.950
to demonstrate, where you can
provide the source as a file

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:36.150
or as a character vector.

00:30:38.840 --> 00:30:43.290
reprex by default
goes and does its work

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:45.870
in the session temp directory.

00:30:45.870 --> 00:30:50.620
That's all part of it
sandboxing all of your work.

00:30:50.620 --> 00:30:55.020
But if your reprex does, for
example, file input and output,

00:30:55.020 --> 00:30:57.840
it could be much
easier to force reprex

00:30:57.840 --> 00:31:01.390
to work in your current
working directory.

00:31:01.390 --> 00:31:07.320
So outfile = NA is
shorthand for that.

00:31:07.320 --> 00:31:13.080
So if I ask R to write the first
six letters of the alphabet

00:31:13.080 --> 00:31:20.040
to a file with outfile
= NA, of a sudden

00:31:20.040 --> 00:31:23.580
these four files that
reprex needs to create

00:31:23.580 --> 00:31:26.020
are being left behind
in my working directory

00:31:26.020 --> 00:31:29.190
instead of in a temp directory.

00:31:29.190 --> 00:31:33.400
And it's the R script
that reprex makes,

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:39.470
it's the HTML that it
uses for the preview,

00:31:39.470 --> 00:31:43.100
and the markdown that it puts
on the clipboard for you.

00:31:43.100 --> 00:31:46.160
So all those usual
files are left behind

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:48.260
in a much more accessible place.

00:31:48.260 --> 00:31:51.200
But you'll notice it has a
godawful filename because we

00:31:51.200 --> 00:31:53.310
just created it out of thin air.

00:31:53.310 --> 00:31:55.730
So if you want to work
somewhere specifically

00:31:55.730 --> 00:32:00.200
and have nice filenames, you
could also provide the base

00:32:00.200 --> 00:32:01.700
for that in outFile.

00:32:04.900 --> 00:32:07.280
And now you see that
it leaves the same four

00:32:07.280 --> 00:32:11.850
files behind but they have
a much better filename.

00:32:11.850 --> 00:32:15.220
So so far I've been
producing reprex output

00:32:15.220 --> 00:32:17.790
that's optimized for GitHub.

00:32:17.790 --> 00:32:21.240
So it's producing what's called
GitHub flavored markdown.

00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:24.930
But Stack Overflow is
another common target,

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:28.405
and that produces slightly
different-looking markdown.

00:32:32.630 --> 00:32:35.740
Stack Overflow doesn't
use fenced code blocks.

00:32:35.740 --> 00:32:38.630
It uses indented code blocks.

00:32:38.630 --> 00:32:41.670
And let me show you what
this would look like.

00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:43.160
I'm going to pretend
like I'm going

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:46.060
to answer my own question
on Stack Overflow,

00:32:46.060 --> 00:32:48.100
but I won't actually
submit this.

00:32:48.100 --> 00:32:50.630
But now if you paste
that into Stack Overflow,

00:32:50.630 --> 00:32:53.390
it also has a preview
feature, and it

00:32:53.390 --> 00:32:56.050
will be formatted correctly
for Stack Overflow.

00:33:00.910 --> 00:33:03.550
You can also make
reprex produce--

00:33:03.550 --> 00:33:06.090
it creates that R script,
which seems sort of weird,

00:33:06.090 --> 00:33:10.815
but an R script that includes
the output as comments.

00:33:10.815 --> 00:33:14.430
And that is very handy
for pasting into an email

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:15.600
or into Slack.

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:20.740
So I'm going to show you
the Slack version of that.

00:33:20.740 --> 00:33:23.190
So this is me talking
to myself on Slack.

00:33:23.190 --> 00:33:26.270
I'm going to create
a code snippet.

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:28.840
Paste it.

00:33:28.840 --> 00:33:32.940
Maybe I always have it set
to R. You create a snippet.

00:33:32.940 --> 00:33:35.970
And that would create a
little R file in Slack

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:37.680
properly syntax highlighted.

00:33:37.680 --> 00:33:41.520
And again, people could copy,
paste it into R and run it,

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:47.577
or sometimes you just
want to inline it.

00:33:47.577 --> 00:33:49.160
You don't get the
syntax highlighting,

00:33:49.160 --> 00:33:52.810
but that also looks quite nice.

00:33:52.810 --> 00:33:58.350
The final venue I'll talk about
is RTF for rich text format.

00:33:58.350 --> 00:34:00.950
And this is a very
experimental venue.

00:34:00.950 --> 00:34:03.960
It only works
probably on the Mac

00:34:03.960 --> 00:34:06.060
at this point because
I actually have to call

00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:08.482
an external utility to do this.

00:34:08.482 --> 00:34:10.440
But I'd like to show you
this, and in fact it's

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:13.510
how I made the
slides for this talk.

00:34:13.510 --> 00:34:16.500
So we run a little
bit of code, but now I

00:34:16.500 --> 00:34:22.187
can go over to Keynote or
PowerPoint or something,

00:34:22.187 --> 00:34:23.270
and I could paste that in.

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:27.800
I'm getting rendered R code that
is properly syntax highlighted.

00:34:27.800 --> 00:34:30.906
And that is, in fact, how all
the snippets in my webinar

00:34:30.906 --> 00:34:31.489
were produced.

00:34:34.230 --> 00:34:39.920
You can suppress the
inclusion of that little ad

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:44.710
at the bottom, or
you can include it.

00:34:44.710 --> 00:34:50.429
You can ask for your reprex
to include session info.

00:34:50.429 --> 00:34:56.070
And for the GitHub venue, it can
be placed in this cute little

00:34:56.070 --> 00:34:57.600
collapsible thing.

00:34:57.600 --> 00:34:59.910
So this is a great
thing to include

00:34:59.910 --> 00:35:02.790
if you think, for example,
that the bug you're recording

00:35:02.790 --> 00:35:05.790
could possibly be related
to the version of software

00:35:05.790 --> 00:35:06.900
on your computer.

00:35:06.900 --> 00:35:08.550
And I love that it
gets folded here.

00:35:08.550 --> 00:35:11.890
So sometimes people include
this when they don't need to,

00:35:11.890 --> 00:35:14.620
and it's kind of overwhelming.

00:35:14.620 --> 00:35:18.495
So the fact that we can
put it in this folding tag

00:35:18.495 --> 00:35:20.950
is really nice.

00:35:20.950 --> 00:35:27.320
reprex can also use the styler
package to restyle your code.

00:35:27.320 --> 00:35:33.160
So here's a really, I would say,
poorly formatted piece of code.

00:35:33.160 --> 00:35:35.860
So by default, reprex
trusts that you

00:35:35.860 --> 00:35:39.550
know what you're hearing and
that you like your formatting.

00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:42.520
But if you don't
trust yourself, you

00:35:42.520 --> 00:35:47.800
can explicitly ask for
reprex to restyle your code

00:35:47.800 --> 00:35:51.730
and give it a much more
conventional layout.

00:35:51.730 --> 00:35:54.595
You can be silly and
change your comment string

00:35:54.595 --> 00:35:59.150
and make it some sort
of emoticon if you want.

00:35:59.150 --> 00:36:05.710
reprex is part of the tidyverse,
so the tidyverse metapackage

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:09.760
can be quite chatty at
start up and tell you

00:36:09.760 --> 00:36:11.810
all the packages that
you've just attached

00:36:11.810 --> 00:36:14.240
and if there are
conflicts between them.

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:19.170
So we actually have a special
argument where you can control

00:36:19.170 --> 00:36:21.390
whether you want that or
not, and usually you don't.

00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:24.450
So we default to silencing it.

00:36:24.450 --> 00:36:25.920
And then the last
thing I'll show

00:36:25.920 --> 00:36:32.340
you is reprex can actually
capture input that,

00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:34.872
in an interactive session,
shows up in your console

00:36:34.872 --> 00:36:36.830
but it's actually being
sent to standard output

00:36:36.830 --> 00:36:38.860
or standard error.

00:36:38.860 --> 00:36:43.020
So I'm going to install
a package from GitHub

00:36:43.020 --> 00:36:45.390
that requires compilation.

00:36:45.390 --> 00:36:48.600
This takes a moment, so
I'm going to chat over it.

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:51.210
But what you're going to see
when this reprex actually

00:36:51.210 --> 00:36:55.680
renders is that we have captured
everything that would normally

00:36:55.680 --> 00:36:58.880
show up in the R
console when doing this,

00:36:58.880 --> 00:37:01.710
so the stuff that is
sort of coming from R

00:37:01.710 --> 00:37:03.490
as well as the
things that are being

00:37:03.490 --> 00:37:08.085
sent standard output
and standard input.

00:37:08.085 --> 00:37:09.710
I was hoping that
would be enough time.

00:37:09.710 --> 00:37:11.020
There we go.

00:37:11.020 --> 00:37:15.770
And so here's the output of
installing the bench package

00:37:15.770 --> 00:37:18.830
from GitHub, which does
require compilation.

00:37:18.830 --> 00:37:21.980
So this is the part that's
coming through sort of normal R

00:37:21.980 --> 00:37:25.940
channels, and this
is capturing what's

00:37:25.940 --> 00:37:28.610
being sent to standard
output and standard input.

00:37:31.160 --> 00:37:35.164
So that was a very quick live
demo of some of the more--

00:37:35.164 --> 00:37:36.830
I don't know if they're
really advanced,

00:37:36.830 --> 00:37:38.621
but features you don't
need in every reprex

00:37:38.621 --> 00:37:41.870
but that you might
need before long.

00:37:41.870 --> 00:37:46.470
A lot of the things that I
showed you toggling on and off,

00:37:46.470 --> 00:37:49.640
you can actually set up your
own personal defaults for these

00:37:49.640 --> 00:37:53.630
things by, again, putting
some code in your .Rprofile,

00:37:53.630 --> 00:37:55.410
which we've already
talked about.

00:37:55.410 --> 00:38:00.080
So this is just an example
of someone who hates the ad.

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:02.660
They always want to
include session info.

00:38:02.660 --> 00:38:05.240
They always want to
restyle their code.

00:38:05.240 --> 00:38:10.460
They have a whimsical sense of
what the comment string should

00:38:10.460 --> 00:38:14.330
be for output, and
they always want

00:38:14.330 --> 00:38:16.200
to see the tidyverse
startup message.

00:38:16.200 --> 00:38:18.920
So these are not my
defaults, but it's

00:38:18.920 --> 00:38:21.750
an example of what you can do.

00:38:21.750 --> 00:38:24.180
And the last thing I'll
show you mechanically

00:38:24.180 --> 00:38:28.590
is most of the time I do
not do what I've shown you,

00:38:28.590 --> 00:38:32.250
which is copying
code to the keyboard

00:38:32.250 --> 00:38:34.820
and then going to the
console and typing reprex.

00:38:34.820 --> 00:38:37.680
There are two RStudio
add-ins that really

00:38:37.680 --> 00:38:41.040
accelerate your reprex life.

00:38:41.040 --> 00:38:45.210
One of them is called Render
reprex, which launches a GUI--

00:38:45.210 --> 00:38:47.130
I'll show you that in a second--

00:38:47.130 --> 00:38:52.290
or reprex selection
literally reprexes the code

00:38:52.290 --> 00:38:56.680
that you have selected, and it's
absolutely conceived for use

00:38:56.680 --> 00:38:59.380
with a keyboard shortcut.

00:38:59.380 --> 00:39:04.570
And RStudio lets you modify
your keyboard shortcuts.

00:39:04.570 --> 00:39:09.550
And so I have bound that
add-in to Shift-Command-R. This

00:39:09.550 --> 00:39:11.350
is how I'd usually use reprex.

00:39:11.350 --> 00:39:14.020
And, for example, Hadley
also uses it a lot.

00:39:14.020 --> 00:39:17.215
He has bound it
to something else.

00:39:17.215 --> 00:39:19.290
And let me go show
you the add-in.

00:39:23.230 --> 00:39:27.030
So again, I could select
the snippet of code

00:39:27.030 --> 00:39:37.080
that made that figure
and launch the add-in.

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:39.720
And so this allows me to
specify a lot of the things

00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:44.400
that you can specify in
the call by clicking.

00:39:44.400 --> 00:39:49.930
So I am going to take the source
from the current selection.

00:39:49.930 --> 00:39:53.230
Let's target Stack Overflow.

00:39:53.230 --> 00:39:56.570
And yes, let's append
the session info.

00:39:56.570 --> 00:40:00.530
Quick Render, and
the usual things

00:40:00.530 --> 00:40:04.910
happen, and the usual
output appears down here.

00:40:04.910 --> 00:40:08.390
And Stack Overflow doesn't
have the capability

00:40:08.390 --> 00:40:10.909
to support this
little folding toggle,

00:40:10.909 --> 00:40:12.950
so the session info actually
gets dumped in there

00:40:12.950 --> 00:40:14.810
in its full glory.

00:40:14.810 --> 00:40:22.860
So these are two other
ways to get your input

00:40:22.860 --> 00:40:25.530
into the reprex function
that actually are probably

00:40:25.530 --> 00:40:29.130
more humane than typing
it in all the time.

00:40:29.130 --> 00:40:30.797
All right, the last
thing I will say--

00:40:30.797 --> 00:40:32.880
I'll try to go quite quickly
because we're already

00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:34.780
at 45 minutes--

00:40:34.780 --> 00:40:38.340
is talking a little bit about
the human side of making

00:40:38.340 --> 00:40:39.480
reproducible examples.

00:40:39.480 --> 00:40:42.540
And now this has nothing to
do with the reprex package.

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:47.025
It's just about asking questions
so they actually get answered.

00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:54.990
And I like this image because
it conveys somehow that we're

00:40:54.990 --> 00:40:56.240
talking about programming.

00:40:56.240 --> 00:40:58.690
Maybe we're all supposed
to be acting like robots.

00:40:58.690 --> 00:41:01.890
And people often seem to assume
that they're talking to robots,

00:41:01.890 --> 00:41:06.240
but there's a lot of humans
involved in this process.

00:41:06.240 --> 00:41:08.850
And I want to warn you,
I'm giving a little bit

00:41:08.850 --> 00:41:10.040
of tough love here.

00:41:10.040 --> 00:41:14.630
There's been a lot of, although
probably still not enough, talk

00:41:14.630 --> 00:41:20.520
of experts being empathetic to
newcomers and question askers.

00:41:20.520 --> 00:41:22.470
But since this is
a talk targeted

00:41:22.470 --> 00:41:25.740
at people asking questions
and comparing examples,

00:41:25.740 --> 00:41:28.650
I also want to say it has to
go the other direction as well.

00:41:28.650 --> 00:41:34.380
So bear with me for a moment
here, but I need to say,

00:41:34.380 --> 00:41:37.490
with all the love in
the world, sometimes

00:41:37.490 --> 00:41:40.250
people come with a
question and they

00:41:40.250 --> 00:41:43.910
have a very rigid theory
about what's wrong

00:41:43.910 --> 00:41:47.250
or how they should
be solving a problem.

00:41:47.250 --> 00:41:50.540
But if your theory about what
was going wrong was so great,

00:41:50.540 --> 00:41:53.320
you wouldn't be here asking
this question right now.

00:41:53.320 --> 00:41:55.520
And this is the origin
of why people really

00:41:55.520 --> 00:42:00.110
want to see code instead
of having sort of a prose

00:42:00.110 --> 00:42:03.710
discussion because it's
very hard sometimes

00:42:03.710 --> 00:42:07.160
to tell what people are
really talking about.

00:42:07.160 --> 00:42:11.720
The other life phenomenon
I want to link this to is--

00:42:11.720 --> 00:42:13.520
I don't know if you've
had this experience,

00:42:13.520 --> 00:42:14.940
but if you've ever
tried to help,

00:42:14.940 --> 00:42:18.200
for example, one of your
relatives sort out a computer

00:42:18.200 --> 00:42:22.670
problem over the phone, it
can be extremely difficult.

00:42:22.670 --> 00:42:25.280
A lot of what they're saying
doesn't really make sense.

00:42:25.280 --> 00:42:29.576
They don't use the words you're
used to to refer to things.

00:42:29.576 --> 00:42:31.950
You just feel like you can't
really get a grip on things.

00:42:31.950 --> 00:42:33.920
And this is basically
what it feels

00:42:33.920 --> 00:42:36.740
like when you're trying to
answer someone's programming

00:42:36.740 --> 00:42:40.130
question just based
on English prose.

00:42:40.130 --> 00:42:43.040
And again, this is
why people constantly

00:42:43.040 --> 00:42:46.460
push you to actually just
show a small piece of code.

00:42:46.460 --> 00:42:50.910
It removes all
sorts of ambiguity.

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:54.860
So let's assume that
everybody, the question

00:42:54.860 --> 00:42:58.320
asker and the question answerer,
is acting in good faith.

00:42:58.320 --> 00:43:01.790
And if they're not, then
they're irrelevant to me.

00:43:01.790 --> 00:43:04.490
So everyone's in good faith.

00:43:04.490 --> 00:43:09.440
It turns out that experts
posting on public sites

00:43:09.440 --> 00:43:14.250
actually are afraid to post
code that doesn't work.

00:43:14.250 --> 00:43:18.770
And so another reason why these
people want to see your code

00:43:18.770 --> 00:43:21.830
is they're not just
reading it and guessing.

00:43:21.830 --> 00:43:24.200
Most of these people are
actually running your code,

00:43:24.200 --> 00:43:27.110
proving that their
proposed solution works,

00:43:27.110 --> 00:43:30.260
and then they post it when they
know that it's safe to do so.

00:43:30.260 --> 00:43:32.350
And this was a big
revelation to me.

00:43:32.350 --> 00:43:35.620
I really used to think that
the people I looked up to

00:43:35.620 --> 00:43:39.300
as experts just knew
all this stuff by heart

00:43:39.300 --> 00:43:41.300
and they were answering
all these questions just

00:43:41.300 --> 00:43:42.620
off the cuff.

00:43:42.620 --> 00:43:44.330
And then it gradually
dawned on me

00:43:44.330 --> 00:43:47.930
that part of why they're
experts or expert behavior

00:43:47.930 --> 00:43:51.680
is that they are constantly
running lots of small examples

00:43:51.680 --> 00:43:53.450
and experiments.

00:43:53.450 --> 00:43:59.510
So sharing your problem in
code is extremely fruitful.

00:43:59.510 --> 00:44:02.660
Making a good reprex
is a lot of work.

00:44:02.660 --> 00:44:07.280
Sometimes you think I can only
show my problem in my R session

00:44:07.280 --> 00:44:09.950
and I haven't restarted
R for seven months

00:44:09.950 --> 00:44:13.310
and it requires the full
data set from my thesis.

00:44:13.310 --> 00:44:15.410
And that is, in fact, true.

00:44:15.410 --> 00:44:18.620
It is a lot of work, but
you're asking other people

00:44:18.620 --> 00:44:21.170
to solve a problem,
and so this is

00:44:21.170 --> 00:44:23.550
part of meeting them halfway.

00:44:23.550 --> 00:44:26.580
But it turns out you get
a lot out of this as well.

00:44:26.580 --> 00:44:28.970
So let's be very selfish.

00:44:28.970 --> 00:44:34.370
If you make a good reprex out
of your hairy, messy problem

00:44:34.370 --> 00:44:38.270
and if you reproduce other
people's problems-- even

00:44:38.270 --> 00:44:40.850
reproducing other people's
problems is a real service,

00:44:40.850 --> 00:44:43.430
and then sometimes you're
going to be able to solve them.

00:44:43.430 --> 00:44:44.960
It turns out this
discipline, it's

00:44:44.960 --> 00:44:50.510
like playing scales or
serving over and over again.

00:44:50.510 --> 00:44:54.470
You actually get better at
programming by doing this.

00:44:54.470 --> 00:44:57.500
And the last selfish
point that I'll make

00:44:57.500 --> 00:45:01.250
is it turns out when you sit
down to make a good reprex out

00:45:01.250 --> 00:45:04.790
of your problem and you
keep it self-contained,

00:45:04.790 --> 00:45:07.830
you strip down your giant hairy
data set to the smallest data

00:45:07.830 --> 00:45:11.240
set that reproduces the
problem, it is amazing

00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:14.240
how often you end up
answering your own question

00:45:14.240 --> 00:45:15.920
in the privacy of
your own home and you

00:45:15.920 --> 00:45:19.640
didn't have to make yourself
vulnerable to other people.

00:45:19.640 --> 00:45:22.910
So this is a great
revelation, and I

00:45:22.910 --> 00:45:26.010
think the reason this works is
that when you have a problem

00:45:26.010 --> 00:45:28.190
it's very easy to just
keep going in circles

00:45:28.190 --> 00:45:30.290
and banging your head
against the desk,

00:45:30.290 --> 00:45:34.290
but there's something about
preparing it for other people--

00:45:34.290 --> 00:45:37.490
and the reprex package is also
being a real hardass about

00:45:37.490 --> 00:45:40.580
making sure that your
problem is self-contained--

00:45:40.580 --> 00:45:44.750
it kind of knocks you out of
that very unproductive place

00:45:44.750 --> 00:45:49.400
and gets you back on the path
of actually working the problem.

00:45:49.400 --> 00:45:51.830
So most people report
this when they first

00:45:51.830 --> 00:45:53.970
start making
reproducible examples

00:45:53.970 --> 00:45:58.790
is that it's kind of amazing
how often this exercise means

00:45:58.790 --> 00:46:01.700
you actually answer
your own question.

00:46:01.700 --> 00:46:04.747
I will skip over this section
since we're out of time

00:46:04.747 --> 00:46:06.580
and I didn't think
people wanted to know it,

00:46:06.580 --> 00:46:08.920
but it's here, a
little bit about what's

00:46:08.920 --> 00:46:11.350
happening behind the scenes.

00:46:11.350 --> 00:46:12.560
And I will conclude.

00:46:12.560 --> 00:46:15.670
I want to give a huge
thank you Yihui Xie

00:46:15.670 --> 00:46:19.870
and all the people who have
brought us the rmarkdown

00:46:19.870 --> 00:46:22.660
package and Pandoc.

00:46:22.660 --> 00:46:27.070
The reprex package is just a
wrapper around those things,

00:46:27.070 --> 00:46:29.470
and I mean that in
both senses, like that

00:46:29.470 --> 00:46:31.780
is literally all that it is.

00:46:31.780 --> 00:46:34.430
So in some sense there's
not much there there.

00:46:34.430 --> 00:46:37.300
But on the other hand
when I first made

00:46:37.300 --> 00:46:40.930
it and especially now that
I've worked on it more,

00:46:40.930 --> 00:46:42.580
there's actually a
lot more going on.

00:46:42.580 --> 00:46:45.010
The friction that it
removes is friction

00:46:45.010 --> 00:46:47.260
that is really important
to remove, I think.

00:46:47.260 --> 00:46:49.800
So in any case, it could not
be possible without rmarkdown

00:46:49.800 --> 00:46:50.800
and Pandoc.

00:46:50.800 --> 00:46:53.920
And, in fact, I should thank
all the coauthors of reprex,

00:46:53.920 --> 00:46:56.570
and there's a lot of people
who have contributed to it

00:46:56.570 --> 00:46:59.080
and a lot of users who
have been extremely

00:46:59.080 --> 00:47:01.840
generous with their bug
reports and feature requests

00:47:01.840 --> 00:47:05.060
and reporting how things
work on different platforms.

00:47:05.060 --> 00:47:08.680
So to conclude, go
forth and engage

00:47:08.680 --> 00:47:15.750
in very precise code-heavy
conversations about R.

00:47:15.750 --> 00:47:21.870
I'm going to take a look
at the questions now.

00:47:21.870 --> 00:47:25.690
Can reprex be able
to also capture

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:28.210
variables in the
environment and include

00:47:28.210 --> 00:47:30.470
in the reproducible example?

00:47:30.470 --> 00:47:37.330
Currently it cannot, and that
is very intentional because we

00:47:37.330 --> 00:47:39.250
really prioritize
the sandboxing.

00:47:41.760 --> 00:47:45.220
I am actually contemplating
basically creating a backdoor

00:47:45.220 --> 00:47:51.050
for this mostly because
of creating RTF snippets,

00:47:51.050 --> 00:47:54.490
but at the moment it cannot,
and that is intentional.

00:47:54.490 --> 00:47:57.910
But I'm open to people
sharing their use

00:47:57.910 --> 00:47:59.890
case about why that should be.

00:47:59.890 --> 00:48:01.270
But basically if you do this--

00:48:04.190 --> 00:48:06.170
yeah, there are
ways you can do it

00:48:06.170 --> 00:48:08.870
that actually make those objects
available to other people

00:48:08.870 --> 00:48:11.200
as well, but it becomes a
much heavier-weight package,

00:48:11.200 --> 00:48:15.050
so so far we have not done that.

00:48:15.050 --> 00:48:18.130
Often times-- this
is a continuation

00:48:18.130 --> 00:48:19.930
of that question--
the issue is buried

00:48:19.930 --> 00:48:22.520
in some part of larger code.

00:48:22.520 --> 00:48:24.400
And rather than going
back to the places

00:48:24.400 --> 00:48:26.410
where that data is
gathering or calculated,

00:48:26.410 --> 00:48:30.460
wouldn't it be nice to grab the
variable in the current state?

00:48:30.460 --> 00:48:32.620
Again, like yes and no.

00:48:32.620 --> 00:48:38.074
I would say extracting that out
is perhaps part of your job.

00:48:38.074 --> 00:48:40.240
Well, I'm now realizing
that some of these questions

00:48:40.240 --> 00:48:41.031
have been answered.

00:48:43.400 --> 00:48:45.860
Does reprex, the
function, actually

00:48:45.860 --> 00:48:48.500
check that the code
is self-contained

00:48:48.500 --> 00:48:51.020
or does it just
generate the template?

00:48:51.020 --> 00:48:53.600
It just generates a template.

00:48:53.600 --> 00:48:58.310
So the way you would find
out that your code is not

00:48:58.310 --> 00:49:02.250
self-contained is
that you get an error.

00:49:02.250 --> 00:49:05.480
But it explicitly allows
errors because sometimes you're

00:49:05.480 --> 00:49:07.580
using reprex to show errors.

00:49:07.580 --> 00:49:13.730
So no, it's on you, the
human, to decide if you're

00:49:13.730 --> 00:49:17.240
getting the result you expect.

00:49:17.240 --> 00:49:21.890
Does readr::read_csv support the
text argument as an alternative

00:49:21.890 --> 00:49:24.870
to file or is that
only in read.csv?

00:49:24.870 --> 00:49:26.990
readr also does this.

00:49:26.990 --> 00:49:32.150
It also supports the inline
creation of a data frame.

00:49:32.150 --> 00:49:34.580
It does it through
its primary argument.

00:49:34.580 --> 00:49:37.480
So you would want to read
the help for read_csv,

00:49:37.480 --> 00:49:41.540
underscores but in fact
it's actually sort of even

00:49:41.540 --> 00:49:44.220
a smaller departure
from your usual use.

00:49:51.210 --> 00:49:54.680
I think I have another instance
of the same question, which

00:49:54.680 --> 00:49:59.730
is could reprex help you
package up an existing data set

00:49:59.730 --> 00:50:02.880
and stick it into your data?

00:50:02.880 --> 00:50:07.110
That reprex dos and don't
document outlines various ways

00:50:07.110 --> 00:50:09.280
of doing this.

00:50:09.280 --> 00:50:13.000
For example, dput
is a great function.

00:50:13.000 --> 00:50:17.670
If you have a slightly awkward
object and you simply cannot

00:50:17.670 --> 00:50:19.270
make your point
without using it,

00:50:19.270 --> 00:50:23.310
dput at least creates a
representation of that that you

00:50:23.310 --> 00:50:25.290
can put into a reprex.

00:50:25.290 --> 00:50:26.680
But so far, you're right.

00:50:26.680 --> 00:50:30.810
I have not made this terribly
easy, and at least the story

00:50:30.810 --> 00:50:38.370
I tell myself is that finding
a really simple object

00:50:38.370 --> 00:50:41.370
is intrinsically part
of the reprex task.

00:50:41.370 --> 00:50:44.190
But this does come up
over and over again.

00:50:44.190 --> 00:50:46.670
Can you do a reprex
for a Shiny app?

00:50:50.400 --> 00:50:51.340
It's not easy.

00:50:51.340 --> 00:50:56.290
I think the Shiny team is or
has developed their own web

00:50:56.290 --> 00:51:01.620
page talking about how
you create Shiny reprexes,

00:51:01.620 --> 00:51:03.680
and they don't use this package.

00:51:03.680 --> 00:51:05.935
So now I'm talking about
reprexes as a concept.

00:51:08.820 --> 00:51:14.900
Let's see, can reprex
produce interactive plots?

00:51:14.900 --> 00:51:17.000
All I will say is
I have not done so.

00:51:20.158 --> 00:51:21.880
So I'll end on this question.

00:51:24.840 --> 00:51:25.430
I don't know.

00:51:25.430 --> 00:51:27.030
It would have to be tried.

00:51:27.030 --> 00:51:29.820
But what I will
say, the slide that

00:51:29.820 --> 00:51:32.430
I skipped over, what
actually happens--

00:51:32.430 --> 00:51:36.930
so currently reprex works
by dumping your R code

00:51:36.930 --> 00:51:40.810
into a templated R script.

00:51:40.810 --> 00:51:46.090
And I have contemplated
revisiting this and dumping it

00:51:46.090 --> 00:51:51.130
into an and rmarkdown
file, and then it

00:51:51.130 --> 00:51:55.300
would be much more capable
in terms of what you can do.

00:51:55.300 --> 00:51:57.820
In some ways there's a lot of
equivalence between rendering

00:51:57.820 --> 00:51:59.620
R scripts and rmarkdown
files, but there

00:51:59.620 --> 00:52:00.860
are some key differences.

00:52:00.860 --> 00:52:03.850
And so I could imagine
that the ability to include

00:52:03.850 --> 00:52:08.710
interactivity is probably
present if you're using

00:52:08.710 --> 00:52:13.446
rmarkdown and could easily
be absent if you're using .R.

00:52:13.446 --> 00:52:16.910
All right, I think
I will leave it

00:52:16.910 --> 00:52:21.190
here and conclude the webinar.

